**Theorem 3.5 (four separate history kernels and lawful transport).**
Separately for each $V\in\{S,B,T,M_J\}$ and $h,k\in\mathcal C$,

$$
V(h)=V(k)\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad h=k.
$$

All twelve mathematical conversions between their actual images exist,
with inverse and triangle equations. They preserve native partial
legality, labels, successors and readouts on actual edges whose endpoints
belong to the stated domains. They do not create new original experiments
or current access permissions.

Proof. If $S(h)=S(k)$, Theorem 3.2 gives equal raw records, hence equal
selected $M_J$; Theorem 3.4 gives $h=k$. If $h=k$, the deterministic
initialized templates give identical supports. This proves both $S$
implications. Equal $B$ gives equal prefix closures and equal raw records,
then the same memory and history; equal history gives the same terminal
paths. This proves both $B$ implications independently. Equal $T$ erases
to equal native histories; equal native histories produce the same
ordered patch occurrences with actual constructor roles. This proves
both $T$ implications. The last two implications are exactly the
extraction and actual-selection directions of Theorem 3.4 for $M_J$.

Support decodes raw backing and selects memory. Prefix closure and
maximality convert boundary and support. Extraction and literal replay
convert memory to occurrences; executing occurrences from the charged
foundation gives support. Each composite returns the corresponding
view of the same $h$, proving the inverse and triangle equations. The
row square preserves the original native domains/labels/readouts and
the same successor, so the induced image edges commute. A raw enabled
exit outside $\mathcal C$ still executes by Theorem 2.4; it is not
assigned an illegal-none response to make this subdomain closed. The
last halt edge is transported although its terminal task kernel need
not be diagonal. ∎

